Explain SQL Server Architecture from client request to result  ؟

When a client submits a T-SQL request, the request is sent to SQL Server through a configured connectivity protocol, 
such as TCP/IP, Named Pipes, or Shared Memory for local connections. SQL Server uses TDS, or Tabular Data Stream,
to exchange SQL Server requests, commands, and results between the client and the server.

Once the request reaches the Database Engine, the Command Parser parses and validates the T-SQL statement. 
The Query Optimizer determines an efficient execution strategy and generates an execution plan. 
SQL Server can store the execution plan in the Plan Cache so it can potentially be reused for subsequent executions. 
The Query Executor then executes the selected plan and accesses the required data. The result is returned to the client through TDS.

SQL OS provides underlying services that support the Database Engine, including scheduling, memory management, synchronization, workers, and tasks.


SO:

Shared Memory / Named Pipes / TCP/IP ⬅️ Connectivity protocols
TDS ⬅️ SQL Server communication/data protocol
Parser / Optimizer / Executor ⬅️ Query processing
SQL OS ⬅️ Internal operating-system-like services inside SQL Server


